Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Wheel Four Releases After the Call
- Story & Context: The debut begins at 112 BPM with Leon and Iris establishing a clean two-bar boogie pocket built from dry kick, snare, closed hats, and a muted octave bass figure. Marit enters only at the end of bar four with a two-note shawm call: open fifth, then flattened seventh. Immediately after the second note, Leon removes the next expected hi-hat and holds the bass for half a beat. That tiny gap becomes the song’s hook.

Each later cycle changes what disappears after the shawm. First the hat vanishes, then the bass anticipation, then the clap, then one kick. Koen marks every fourth release with a damped bass-marimba note instead of the missing electronic event. Soraya waits until the shawm tail has fully stopped before answering with a muted low third.

At the midpoint, Marit overblows the upper note into a rough partial. The groove does not become louder; instead, the following brake window expands to a full beat, making the high reed pressure seem to have physically slowed the rhythm. The final cycle returns to the original half-beat release and ends with the drum machine continuing alone.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Night industrial test hall with an old tram wheel labeled 4 suspended above a roller rig, yellow hazard line crossing the floor, dark shawm resting on a brake-pressure chart, CP-70 keys and marimba mallet in shallow focus. No full lyrics; optional spoken fragments limited to “wheel four,” “release after the call,” “hold half a beat,” and “resume the line.”

Track 2: Pressure Lamp Stays Amber
- Story & Context: The second track uses the warmest harmony on the EP. Leon programs a 108 BPM groove with a Dorian CP-70 add9 voicing struck only once every two bars. Marit plays a single mid-register shawm note before each chord, never during it. The instrument’s unstable reed onset therefore becomes a kind of pickup into a rigid electric-piano landing.

Iris keeps the bass almost motionless for the first minute, repeating tonic, octave, and one chromatic approach. Koen substitutes bass-marimba notes for the kick in every eighth bar. Soraya uses cup mute and breathy attacks to answer the shawm with descending low-fifth fragments.

The track’s central rule is that the amber pressure-status lamp sampled from the hall is never allowed to become a snare or rhythmic loop. It appears only as a faint electrical hum under the CP-70 sustain, creating continuity without adding percussion. During the final section, Marit stops overblowing entirely and plays softer, lower phrases, making the groove feel larger because less upper-mid energy is present.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Analog maintenance console with one amber status lamp glowing steadily, faded brake-force graph beneath it, shawm reed and CP-70 tuning hardware on the desk, muted trombone reflected in scratched glass. Spoken fragments limited to “amber only,” “pressure holding,” “do not trip red,” and “keep the wheel turning.”

Track 3: The Groove Passes the Inspection Pit
- Story & Context: The closer begins at 116 BPM with the most active bass line on the record: a clipped octave figure with sixteenth-note anticipations that never changes for the first thirty-two bars. Marit stays silent until the listener has fully internalized the loop. Her first entry is a single short shawm note, followed by a complete one-beat brake window where every pitched instrument drops out but the snare remains.

Koen then introduces bass-marimba strokes beneath the returning groove, alternating with the electronic kick rather than doubling it. Soraya plays two-note muted-trombone answers that gradually move farther away from Marit’s pitch. The harmonic field opens while the rhythm remains unchanged.

In the final minute, the ensemble walks physically toward the inspection pit while continuing to play. The shawm’s room reflection changes slightly as Marit crosses from tile to open pit space. Leon removes the programmed brake windows completely and lets the groove run uninterrupted for sixteen bars. The last shawm call therefore receives no release at all, revealing how essential the earlier absences had become.
